ou're trying to  accomplish they're aligned right they  love what this company is all about  beyond just making money and they share  the same values this is so important  this is one thing that people almost  never look at you have to just like  getting married you have to be with  somebody who has the same values as you  because most of your fights are going to  be around values related stuff and if  you  share different values it's not going to  work you can't get somebody to change  what their core values are it's better  to align yourself with people who have  the same core values as you and you have  the complementary skill set so there's a  lot of work that you have to do right up  front to make sure that you're bringing  in the right partners from the start  number two is have defined rules so you  may not be able to choose your partners  now that you already have your three  business partners but you can have very  clearly defined roles you have to know  who's doing what I've no idea what your  company does I've no idea what  everybody's responsibilities are but you  have to set that up you have to know  that you're in charge of this this guy's  in charge of this she's in charge of  that you have clearly defined roles and  you don't step on each other's toes have  a job description and somebody's got to  be the CEO but you don't have a four  headed monster at the top of this thing  pick one person who's the best person to  lead this business forward that's your  president that's your CEO everybody else  has their roles they have the  responsibilities and you're not stepping  on each other's toes otherwise it's  mayhem you're never going to agree  you're always going to be fighting over  what to do and that means no progress  for the company  number three pay for performance and  split the profits so you're asking about  how do you how do you get paid how do  you decide who's worth what enticed with  the profits I'm assuming you're equal  partners right you for your equal  partners in the business what you want  to do here is you get paid in two ways  one you have equity right so if you're  going to give a dividend to the owners  or if you're going to have a management  bonus or if you're going to have profit  sharing that should be equal right it  should be based on how many shares you  have if if somebody owns twice as many  shares as somebody else amongst your  four partners then that person gets  twice as much of a dividend when you're  sharing those profits at the end of the  year or however you're going to do it  right so the profit sharing is equal but  what doesn't have to be equal  necessarily is the performance based pay  so you want to make sure that everybody  that is on the team is getting paid  right the Presidents getting paid you're  developers are getting paid your market  birds are getting the paid however you  split up your four roles everybody is  getting  and typically what companies will do is  figure out how much would it cost to  replace that person about to go in hire  so I'm going to come out to be that  person to be that lead programmer to be  that marketing director to be that CEO  what would it cost me to find a  replacement salary for and that's how  much that person gets paid now at the  start of a new business you don't have a  lot of money you're not raking it in yet  so quite often what companies will do is  everybody gets the same pay everybody  gets the same profit chairs until you  start making some real money and then  people start to fight right when there's  no money when the company's not making  anything you don't have fights over  who's taking what because there's not  enough but when your company starts to  make money then people start feeling  like hey I'm worth more I'm adding more  value here  I should be earning a higher salary and  what you can do is look at what that  person is bringing and what real cost to  replace them so everybody can pretend  you have a different salary if people  are bringing different value if it's  more expensive to replace one person in  another but if you're owning equal  shares then you split the profits  equally number four is have a tiebreaker  sometimes people fight and you'll have  partners who just do not agree on  something and you need to have somebody  who can be that tiebreaker for you so  you're in a situation where you have  four partners right so you might have  three partners against one or or four  against none which is great but the  problem comes what have two people agree  on one side and two people agree on the  other side how do you how do you solve  that what a lot of companies will do is  they have an advisor who will be a part  of the company and own 1% of the shares  and that person is called on to split  the vote right so it might be your  accountant there might be a lawyer  potentially a banker somebody who can  add value to your business who can give  you advice it could be a coach it could  be a mentor could be a business person  you look up to who owns a really small  piece of the company and they can come  on board if you guys are ever in a  deadlock that you can't get past because  you need to make a decision to move on  you will not have success just  constantly fighting amongst each other  and so a lot of companies who are in  partnerships will have that person set  up as a type  and number five is have a great  shoulders agreement as sad as it is to  say you know I'm the guy who believes  but as sad as it is the same most  partnerships don't work out and now  you're talking about having four people  in a partnership it's probably not going  to work out with everybody staying on  board for years and years if you look at  the history of companies it's very very  very rare that happens and so what you  want to do is make sure you have a great  shareholders agreement and what it does  is outlines the rules for coming in or  leaving a partnership so for example one  of the causes you want to have is as a  shotgun agreement so you need the  shotgun clause you've got to have that  in a basically what it means is you can  if somebody's being a big pain you know  you just cannot get along with this  other partner or two partners or par you  know that one of them has to leave they  can invoke the shotgun clause and what  it does is basically says either I'm in  or you're in and I set the price I think  the value of my shares are worth this  amount of money so you can either buy me  for that amount of money or I'm going to  buy you for that same amount of money  and they have to make the decision and  somebody's in and somebody's out and  they set the rules and this way you're  not going to court and you're not  continuing to fight and it doesn't cause  a whole bunch of issues but a good  charles greane will also outline other  issues so for example if if somebody  buys you out right same somebody wants  to acquire your business do they buy out  all the other partners or not or can  they just buy out too and on 50% of the  shares right does everybody have to  agree if somebody's selling the shares  can I go sell my shares to my best  friend who's now going to own part of  your business is that okay  right or do you have right of first  refusal if I die do my shares go to my  wife right and then she's a partner in  your business is that okay or do you  have right of first refusal right all of  these things can be figured out because  chances are somebody's going to have a  problem with the business and it may be  easy to solve but typically it's not and  so having a good shows agreement will  save you aggravation  money and help your business continue to  grow so in summary how to deal with  difficult partners number one have  aligned vision and values number two  have defined roles  number three pay for performance split  the profits number four have a  tiebreaker  and number five have a great  shareholders agreement and believe for  those who are watching like the video  you can subscribe to the channel to see  more just like this thank you so much  for watching  I'll see you soon don't forget to check  out my newsletter click the link below  hi I'm Jason Grif on seeker of razz  online comm and I want to say Evan  Carmichael's newsletter is consistent  fun inspiring and XD only newsletter I  prioritize and look forward to period
 

